# Introduction to Algorithms: Thinking in Steps

> An algorithm is a finite, unambiguous sequence of steps that solves a
> problem. Recipes, directions, and long-division procedures are all
> algorithms — computer science just makes the idea precise.

## 1. What Makes an Algorithm?

A valid algorithm must be:

1. **Finite** — it eventually stops.
2. **Unambiguous** — every step is precisely defined.
3. **Effective** — each step is actually doable.
4. **General** — it works for a whole class of inputs, not one case.

[definition]
**Correctness** means the algorithm produces the right output for every
valid input. **Efficiency** asks how much time and memory it needs as
the input grows. A slow correct algorithm beats a fast wrong one — but
usually we want both.
[/definition]

## 2. Measuring Cost: Big-O Notation

We count how running time grows with input size *n*, ignoring constants:

| Complexity | Name | Example problem |
| --- | --- | --- |
| O(1) | Constant | Accessing an array element |
| O(log n) | Logarithmic | Binary search |
| O(n) | Linear | Scanning a list once |
| O(n log n) | Linearithmic | Merge sort |
| O(n²) | Quadratic | Nested loops comparing all pairs |
| O(2ⁿ) | Exponential | Trying every subset |

## Example: linear search vs binary search

Finding a name in a sorted list of 1,000,000 entries:

- **Linear search** checks up to 1,000,000 names.
- **Binary search** halves the range each step — about 20 checks,
  since log₂(1,000,000) ≈ 20.

@diagram flowchart id="binary-search-flow" layout="hierarchical" aria="The binary search decision loop"
node start label="Compare middle item" shape="pill"
node eq label="Match?" shape="diamond"
node left label="Search lower half" shape="round-rect"
node right label="Search upper half" shape="round-rect"
node done label="Found it" shape="round-rect" fill="#22c55e"
node fail label="Range empty → absent" shape="round-rect"

start -> eq
eq -> done label="yes" color="#22c55e"
eq -> left label="target smaller"
eq -> right label="target larger"
left -> start style="dashed"
right -> start style="dashed"
start ..> fail label="if range empties first"
@/diagram

[important]
Growth rate dominates hardware. An O(n²) algorithm on a fast machine
loses to an O(n log n) algorithm on a slow one once lists get big.
[/important]

## 3. Sorting: One Problem, Many Algorithms

| Algorithm | Idea | Average time |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Bubble sort | Swap adjacent out-of-order pairs repeatedly | O(n²) |
| Insertion sort | Place each item into the sorted part so far | O(n²), fast when nearly sorted |
| Merge sort | Split in half, sort halves, merge | O(n log n) |
| Quicksort | Partition around a pivot, recurse | O(n log n) typical |

Merge sort illustrates **divide and conquer**: split the problem,
solve the pieces, combine the results.

## 4. Pseudocode

Algorithms are written in pseudocode before code:

```
BINARY-SEARCH(A, target):
  low  = 0
  high = length(A) - 1
  while low <= high:
    mid = (low + high) / 2   (integer division)
    if A[mid] == target: return mid
    else if A[mid] < target: low  = mid + 1
    else:                    high = mid - 1
  return NOT FOUND
```

## Key Takeaways

- Algorithms must be finite, unambiguous, effective, and general.
- Big-O describes growth, not raw speed.
- Divide-and-conquer turns hard problems into manageable halves.
